## What the gate found (RELAYED from the gate STATUS, read whole by the drafter)
Gate STATUS: `/Volumes/Laptop-DEV/!CODING/Datasec/Datasec MPS Commercial Calculator/1_Project_Definition/Briefs/2026-10-23_B534_STATUS.md` (339 lines; Findings from line 255). Evidence: `.../Briefs/2026-10-23_B534_evidence/` (read it; the files named below). Verdict: **NO GO round 1 of 2 on #100 @ `2f536f2`**, majors+: B534-1, B534-3, B534-4. Everything else the gate checked held: delta 9 paths, 6 commits, three `src` files, 18 of 18 removed lines accounted, exactly one new user-facing sentence (approved), CI line green (Api 2001, Engine 394, Import 162, web 126 files / 1191 tests, 0 failed, 0 skipped), 9 of 9 check-runs success, CodeQL 0 open alerts and 4 of 4 analyses, 35 of 41 source mutants killed, no defective version made live, no second Active, no registry change on any refusal.
- **B534-1 (Major).** `SourceDocumentChecks.cs:136` (`IdKey`), the table `:159-213`, compared at `:85-86` and `:89-90`. `IdKey` composes a letter plus one combining mark into the precomposed character only when its table holds that exact letter. The table holds, for six pairs, one case only: `t`+U+0308 (U+1E97), `w`+U+030A (U+1E98), `y`+U+030A (U+1E99), `h`+U+0331 (U+1E96), `j`+U+030C (U+01F0), all lower case only, and `I`+U+0307 (U+0130), upper case only. So the lower-case spelling composes and the upper-case one does not (or the reverse for `I`), and plain `OrdinalIgnoreCase` on the raw strings says "same" but `IdKey` then `OrdinalIgnoreCase` says "different" (`06d_c6-case-pairs-raw-vs-IdKey.txt`: six pure case pairs; and U+0130 versus `i`+U+0307, U+1E97 versus `T`+U+0308 are false both ways). Repro: a registry holding an Active version whose devices source id is `x-T`+U+0308; validate a Draft copy whose devices id is `x-t`+U+0308: no `DUPLICATE_SOURCE_VERSION_ID`, the copy validates. Within one document likewise. Base accepted every case twin, so it is not weaker than base; it is the product owner's ruling not made true for one class. Done-when MPSCALC-189 (case-twin clause) is PARTLY met only for this class.
- **B534-2 (Minor).** `ReferenceDocumentValidator.cs:211-214` (`SuppliesCppAgrees`) judges a supply whose price is a negative number, against `:131-135` (the `INVALID_SUPPLY_PRICE` arm) and the doc comment `:190-194` ("one defect is one finding"). An included supply with a negative price and a stored cost per page negated to match answers `INVALID_SUPPLY_PRICE@supplyRows[0]`, `CPP_INCONSISTENT@deviceRows[0]`, `CPP_INCONSISTENT@deviceRows[1]` (evidence `05_S7-cpp-hand-head.txt`); base answers the first only. The document still ends Failed Validation.
- **B534-3 (Major).** `ReferenceDocumentValidator.cs:219` (`return d.MonoSuppliesCpp == mono && d.ColourSuppliesCpp == colour;`). Mutant g3 (a tolerance of 0.0001 on both figures) survives the whole Api (2001) and Import (162) projects. The code is exact (the gate's scratch property caught deltas of 1e-28 and 1e-6); the committed rows are not: the one-cent row edits by 0.01, and the seeded property's deltas are multiples of 0.0001, none exactly 0.0001.
- **B534-4 (Major).** `SourceDocumentChecks.cs:129-132` (`if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(id)) return "";`), used at `:84` and `:89`. Mutants d1 (`IdKey(null)` returns the input, so null stays null) and d2 (`IdKey` throws on null or empty) survive all 2001 Api rows. The input is reachable: a document whose devices and options ids are both empty stages 201 (the staging check refuses a null id only), and the head answers validate 200 Failed Validation `DUPLICATE_SOURCE_VERSION_ID@options.version.id` (evidence `08c_c4-empty-ids-head.txt`), while under d2 it answers 500 `SOURCE_LIFECYCLE_FAILED` (`08d_c4-empty-ids-under-d2.txt`). d1 differs only for a null registry id, which a hand-edited registry can hold (the gate did not drive it).
- **B534-5 (Minor).** `SourceLifecycle.cs:528-542` (the order of the repeated-name, engine-read and content checks) and `:541`/`:874-876` (`Defective`). Every `B520_192` row asserts the code `SOURCE_DOCUMENT_INVALID` and the status only. Mutant h6 (`Defective` answers `Unreadable`'s sentence) and mutant h5 (the content check moved before the repeated-name and engine-read checks, so a document that also fails those answers the new sentence) both survive all 220 `SourceLifecycle` rows. The accept/refuse decision is unchanged in both: a sentence-pin gap, not a decision survivor.
